    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_context` in `app/context.py` and added tests for it; all 8 tests pass.\n\n```python\ndocs = retriever.invoke(question, config={\"tags\": [TRACE_TAG]})\n```\n\n- **Works with any retriever:** it only calls the standard `invoke()` method, so the vector-store retriever in production works the same way as `KeywordRetriever`.\n- **Trace tag:** `TRACE_TAG` is passed in the call's config, so it's attached to the lookup's trace entry. A test checks this by recording the trace entries the lookup produces.\n- **Output:** it skips any document whose `source` has already appeared and keeps the retriever's order. It stops at `MAX_SOURCES` entries. Each entry is `[n] <source>` followed by the document text with surrounding whitespace removed, and entries are separated by one blank line. If nothing is found, it returns `\"\"`.\n\nNo dependencies were added.\n\n**Tests:** the new tests are in `tests/test_context.py`. They cover one entry per source in the retriever's order, whitespace removal, the `MAX_SOURCES` limit, the empty result, and the trace tag.\n\n**Docs check:** Context7 was unavailable (its monthly quota was used up), so I checked the API against the installed `langchain_core` 1.6.5 source instead. That's where I confirmed `invoke()` reads its tags from `config[\"tags\"]`."
  },
